\begin{theorem}[Temperature labels and heat processes]
  \label{thm:physics:IPhO_2026_3_C_1:target}
  \lean{IPhO2026Problems.ProblemIPhO2026_3_C_1.identify_temperature_labels_and_heat_processes}
  \uses{def:physics:IPhO_2026_3_C_1:aux007, def:physics:IPhO_2026_3_C_1:aux008, def:physics:IPhO_2026_3_C_1:aux013, def:physics:IPhO_2026_3_C_1:aux014, def:physics:IPhO_2026_3_C_1:aux016, def:physics:IPhO_2026_3_C_1:aux018, def:physics:IPhO_2026_3_C_1:aux019, def:physics:IPhO_2026_3_C_1:aux020, def:physics:IPhO_2026_3_C_1:aux021, lem:physics:IPhO_2026_3_C_1:aux022}
  In the directed cycle, states 1 and 4 have the hot-reservoir
  temperature, states 2 and 3 have the cold-reservoir temperature,
  the torus absorbs the cold heat on \(2\to3\), and it delivers the hot
  heat on \(4\to1\).  The two adiabatic legs carry no heat.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
  First use the reservoir-identification lemma to assign the cold reservoir
  to \(2\to3\) and the hot reservoir to \(4\to1\).  Endpoint equilibrium on
  those contacts gives the four temperature labels.  The general
  reservoir-transfer law gives the two directed heat processes.  Finally,
  the figure marks \(1\to2\) and \(3\to4\) as adiabatic; the isolation and
  no-contact laws therefore give zero heat transfer on both.
\end{proof}

